From 10a4a10fcfe509e9b5a3f9da7ea95eedb2292b91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rj <rj@hermes.local>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 00:49:56 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 1/9] serve: reuse a prompt's common prefix instead of reading
 all of it again

The engine took two checkpoints - the end of the last prompt and the position before its
last token - which is exactly enough for "the next turn re-renders this one" and nothing
else. Every other prompt, however slightly it diverged, reported 0 reused and paid for the
whole read: a 13k prompt whose last sentence changed cost the same as one sharing nothing.

Now prompt-path runs leave a checkpoint each, in a rotating ring of 14 slots above those two
(kCheckpoints = 16), and a request restores the deepest one its prompt still shares. The
marks sit on boundaries the run loop already has, so one costs a ~3 MB host copy and a
stream sync - no read, no extra kernel.

Measured on 13,029 tokens (one engine session per leg, this box):

  full read, nothing shared                 186.6 s
  the same prompt again (next turn)           0.1 s   13,028 reused
  same 13k prefix, new last sentence         77.2 s   8,192 reused  2.4x
  one sentence changed at 55%:
    marks at chunk boundaries (8192)        203.7 s   nothing reusable
    marks every 2048 tokens                 117.9 s   6,144 reused  1.7x

Two things this needed:

- Model::feed_chunk(), because feed_unit() returns max(chunk, max_context) - the whole
  prompt - so the first version of this took no marks at all for any prompt under 64k and
  reported reused=0 while looking healthy. serve cuts runs at the chunk size, or at
  DS4_PREFIX_STRIDE when that is smaller.
- A default run cap of 4096 tokens (DS4_PREFIX_STRIDE's default). A single feed of a whole
  prompt is bimodal on a 13k prompt in a way that reads as a 40% slowdown: 187-191 s when
  the page cache already held its experts, 318-328 s when it did not, always with "CPU lane
  0.5% of the experts" - a few expert calls served from the CPU lane serialize a pipelined
  chunk. Runs of 2048-4096 tokens measured 187-205 s in every state.

The restore is exact: with --no-expert-cache, so that the engine itself reproduces a prompt
bit for bit, a prompt restored from the 8192 mark produced logits bit-identical to reading
it from scratch (max |diff| 0.0 over all 129,280 rows, same argmax). Note that the engine is
not bit-reproducible with the expert cache on - up to ~1.8 logits for the same prompt with
no reuse - so a logit comparison at that scale proves nothing either way.

DS4_PREFIX_REUSE=0 restores the old behaviour exactly.

(cherry picked from commit ffe1bc76d4edb61be4205b6d7760c372dc3a5f2a)
(cherry picked from commit 78bbd7a6698cc789c6e705c4096b572fe1e1db34)
(cherry picked from commit bb968d95ba3be4c0cb0dc66a8d75b58c0fe6658c)
